Blake, I reviewed the uploaded Asterion project as an actual codebase, not just as an idea. My honest read: this is a strong project with a genuinely promising learning-product core, but it is not yet a full P3 A*/top-band trainer. It is much closer to “excellent prototype / colleague-review candidate” than “ready to put in front of all students as a complete Paper 3 exam-prep system.”

The best thing about it is that the project already makes the right moral and pedagogical decision: canonical exam question images and mark-scheme images remain the academic source of truth, while generated content is treated as support. That is exactly the right foundation for CAIE 9709.

The biggest weakness is coverage and measurement. The Content Lab is strongest in Algebra, Logs, and Trig, but P3 success depends on broad, repeatable competence across all Pure 3 topics, especially the large and high-yield areas that are still thin: parametric equations, complex numbers, vectors, numerical methods, differentiation/integration, and differential equations.

Current state of the project

I unpacked the zip, inspected the docs, source, data, tests, and Content Lab pipeline. The app is a Vite/React/TypeScript local-first trainer. It has onboarding, a P3 Astral Academy world map, nine P3 regions, Field Guides, Quick Checks, Warm-up Practice, Training Grounds, Guardian checks, local progress, self-marking, avatar/class hall features, teacher export, and data-health reporting.

The project’s own vision is very coherent: “Understand the idea → try a small check → practice safely → face the Guardian.” That is a good learning loop. It is also aligned with the CAIE P3 exam structure: Cambridge’s 2026–2027 9709 syllabus lists Paper 3 topics as algebra, logs/exponentials, trigonometry, differentiation, integration, numerical solution of equations, vectors, differential equations, and complex numbers. Paper 3 is 1 hour 50 minutes, 75 marks, 9–11 structured questions, and compulsory for A Level. Importantly, Paper 3 is weighted 45% AO1 and 55% AO2, so students need more than recall: they need procedure selection, combined techniques, and clear logical communication.  ￼

A small but important wording point: there is no A grade for Paper 3 alone*. Cambridge says grade A* does not exist as a component grade; A* is calculated for the whole qualification option. So the project should phrase the goal as “A*/top-band readiness for 9709, starting with consistently high Paper 3 performance,” not “an A* on P3.” Cambridge’s threshold page also makes clear that thresholds change by series and are set for fairness across papers.  ￼

Engineering-wise, the build is healthy after reinstalling dependencies. The zip included platform-specific node_modules, so the first build/test environment needed dependency repair. After npm install, npm run build passed. The Content Lab verifier also passed after restoring .git, reporting 34 reviewed teaching snippets, 34 Quick Checks, 30 reviewed generated warm-ups, and 10 generator families.

The data state is strong but incomplete: I found 396 P3 questions, of which 385 are trainable. The remaining 11 are quarantined because 33autumn25 mark-scheme assets are missing or mismatched. That quarantine is a good sign; the system is refusing to pretend incomplete academic data is usable.

The P3 bank is broad: 37 paper/session codes. Topic distribution is uneven, though. In the P3 bundle I found roughly:

