That exposes the main problem: the exam-bank evidence is rich, but the teaching support is not yet distributed in proportion to P3 need. Parametric equations alone has the largest source-question footprint, yet it has almost no Content Lab learning depth.

Main strengths

The first major strength is academic fidelity. The project’s rules are exactly right: canonical question images and mark-scheme images are the source of truth; generated explanations are support only; no browser-side generation; no silent AI marking; no fake mastery. This protects both student trust and teacher trust.

The second strength is the learning loop architecture. Field Guide, Quick Check, Warm-up, canonical practice, and Guardian is a sensible flow. It gives students a route from understanding to performance, rather than just dumping past-paper questions in front of them.

The third strength is self-marking design. The PracticeView flow requires reveal-before-save, gives M/B/A mark breakdown inputs, asks for reflection/mistake tags, and requires an evidence note for full-score claims. That is much better than “enter your score and move on.” It nudges students toward mark-scheme literacy.

The fourth strength is verification discipline. The Content Lab has reviewed/public filters, verification status, source traceability, and a guard against mutating the canonical question_bank.json. This is exactly the kind of boring infrastructure that makes a classroom tool credible.

The fifth strength is teacher-readiness thinking. Local-first storage, teacher export, issue reporting, and data-health checks are all useful for an early classroom pilot. A lot of edtech prototypes ignore the teacher workflow; this one does not.

The sixth strength is that the project avoids the common gamification trap. The RPG layer is presentational and motivational, but mastery is still tied to marks and evidence. That matters.

Main weaknesses

The most important weakness is coverage imbalance. Algebra, logs, and trig are in a decent first-batch state. The other regions are too thin for a full P3 learning product. The current Content Lab report shows no deterministic warm-ups for complex numbers, calculus/parametric work, vectors, numerical methods, or differential equations. Integration has some partial-fractions support, but not enough true integration learning coverage.

This matters because the app’s mission is not “make some nice lessons”; it is “put students in the best place to score highly on P3.” For that, every high-frequency P3 skill needs a worked-example path, quick retrieval, generated warm-ups, canonical practice, and delayed review.

The second weakness is that Quick Checks and Warm-ups are currently reveal-based, not fully practice-based. The UI shows prompts and revealable answers/solutions, but it does not yet make the student enter an answer, compare, log confidence, or route future practice based on the result. That means some of the best learning moments are not yet captured as evidence.

The third weakness is that the adaptive engine is still shallow. It selects by topic weakness, recent attempts, rough difficulty, marks, and recent-question avoidance. That is useful, but not enough. A serious P3 trainer needs sub-skill mastery, error-type routing, spacing, interleaving, and exam-readiness logic.

For example, “integration” is too broad. The app needs to know whether the student failed because they chose the wrong method, made an algebraic simplification error, forgot limits, mishandled partial fractions, lost a constant, or failed to communicate the substitution. Those are different repairs.

The fourth weakness is a concrete progression issue: in regionProgress.ts, calculateRegionProgress passes hasMixedReview: false into calculateRegionRank. Since “Mastered” requires mixed review, the Mastered rank appears effectively blocked in the current region-progress pathway. Guardian-cleared state exists separately, but the rank logic needs fixing.

The fifth weakness is that assessment-outcome measurement is not yet strong enough. Current local attempt marks are useful, but they are not the same as exam readiness. The platform needs a held-out, timed, unseen-question measurement mode. The outcome metric should be: “Can the student score highly on unseen, timed, canonical Paper 3-style questions across the full syllabus?”

The sixth weakness is test/CI ergonomics. Build and Content Lab verification passed, and many targeted unit/component tests passed individually. But the full npm test run did not complete cleanly under the tool timeout, and the root Content Lab pipeline test appears to need isolation/debugging. Before colleagues collaborate seriously, the test suite needs to be fast, reliable, and easy to run.

The seventh weakness is deployment/shareability. The zip was very large, partly because it included node_modules, dist, .git, and large public assets. The current project is fine as a local prototype, but student/classroom deployment will require decisions about privacy, auth, asset access, canonical-paper licensing/permissions, export/delete behavior, and hosting.

What the research suggests you should build

The core learning model should be: worked example → faded example → independent attempt → feedback → spaced/interleaved review → timed exam performance.

Worked examples are especially valuable for students who are still building schemas. Cognitive load research finds that worked examples reduce ineffective load from weak problem-solving strategies and help novice learners focus on the solution procedure. That strongly supports your Field Guide and worked-example direction.  ￼

Retrieval practice needs to be central. Roediger and Karpicke’s testing-effect work found that prior testing produced substantially better delayed retention than repeated studying, even when repeated studying increased students’ confidence. Dunlosky et al. also rated practice testing and distributed practice as high-utility learning techniques.  ￼

Interleaving matters once students have basic fluency. Taylor and Rohrer found that interleaved math practice could impair practice-session performance but improve later test performance, apparently by helping students choose the right procedure for the problem. That is especially relevant to P3, where students often lose marks not because they know no method, but because they choose the wrong method.  ￼

Feedback needs to be specific and actionable. The EEF summarizes feedback as high-impact when it focuses on the task, subject, and self-regulation strategies, and when it gives specific information on how to improve. Your self-marking system is a strong start, but it should evolve into “mark-scheme coaching”: which line earned M1, which line earned A1, which missing statement lost the mark, and what to try next.  ￼

Metacognition should be built into the product, not added as a generic reflection box. The EEF emphasizes planning, monitoring, and evaluating learning in the normal curriculum task, not as a separate “thinking skills” lesson. In Asterion, that means every serious attempt should ask: “What method did you choose?”, “Where did the first mark come from?”, “What will you do differently next time?”  ￼

For motivation, the game layer should support competence, autonomy, and relatedness. Self-determination theory research argues that autonomy-supportive environments and students’ needs for autonomy, competence, and relatedness support engagement, self-regulation, academic performance, and well-being. So the map/avatar/class hall features are useful only if they make students feel, “I know where I am, I know what to do next, and my effort is visible.” They should not become reward noise.  ￼

Product direction I recommend

Do not start by adding more game mechanics. Do not start by adding AI marking. Do not start by making it prettier.

Start by making Asterion a high-integrity P3 learning engine.

The product should become:

“Students learn one P3 micro-skill, retrieve it, apply it in generated warm-up form, apply it to canonical exam questions, self-mark against the real mark scheme, repair the exact weakness, and later prove retention in mixed timed practice.”

That means the Content Lab is not just a content generator. It should become the curriculum engine.

Roadmap

Phase 0 should be engineering stabilization. Fix the full test suite, add reliable CI-style scripts, and clean up the packaged project. Add separate commands like test:unit, test:pipeline, test:ci, and verify:content-lab. Fix the Content Lab pipeline test that hangs or runs too slowly. Also fix the hasMixedReview: false issue so Mastered rank can actually be earned when the evidence exists.

Phase 1 should be P3 coverage parity. Create a P3 skill map that breaks every syllabus topic into micro-skills. For each region, aim for a minimum viable learning set: at least 5–8 teaching snippets, 5–8 Quick Checks, 2–4 deterministic generator families, 9–12 warm-ups, canonical question links, and at least one Guardian candidate. Prioritize by exam-bank evidence, not by what is easiest to generate. The urgent topics are parametric equations, complex numbers, vectors, numerical methods, and calculus/integration/differential equations.

Phase 2 should make Quick Checks and Warm-ups interactive. Students should enter an answer before reveal. The app should log whether they attempted it, whether they revealed early, their confidence, and the error type. These should not inflate exam mastery, but they should guide readiness and next action. Reveal-only content is useful; answer-first content is much better.

Phase 3 should upgrade adaptivity. Move from topic-level routing to sub-skill and error-type routing. Add spacing and interleaving. For example, after a student learns integration by parts, the app should later mix it with substitution, partial fractions, trig identities, and parametric differentiation so the student learns to choose the method, not just execute the method when told.

Phase 4 should add exam-readiness measurement. Create a timed P3 readiness mode using held-out canonical questions. Track marks per topic, marks per minute, first-attempt accuracy, self-marking accuracy, AO2-style procedure selection, and two-week retention. The key score should be something like “P3 Readiness Index,” not XP. It should be conservative.

Phase 5 should prepare colleague collaboration. Add a teacher-review workflow for snippets, Quick Checks, generated warm-ups, and source links. Make it easy for colleagues to approve, reject, edit, or flag content. The review system should preserve provenance: which source question pattern inspired it, which mark-scheme move it trains, and what human checked it.

Phase 6 should prepare student release. Only then worry about hosted deployment, class accounts, privacy, data export/delete, permissions around canonical assets, and classroom analytics.

