What to build first for students

For the first implementation with real students, I would not release the whole “P3 world” as if it is complete. I would pilot one of two ways.

The safer pilot is three-region pilot: Algebra Vault, Logarithm Observatory, and Trigonometry Spire. These are the best-supported regions right now. You can test motivation, self-marking, Field Guide usefulness, and whether students actually complete the loop.

The more valuable exam pilot is parametric equations expansion first. Since parametric equations are the largest P3 source-question cluster in your bank, building that region deeply would likely give the biggest P3-specific payoff. But it needs serious Content Lab work before it is student-ready.

My recommendation: stabilize the project, then run a colleague review on Algebra/Logs/Trig while Codex builds the P3 skill-map and starts the parametric/complex/vector generator expansion.

What I need from you before a deeper implementation pass

I can go further with the project right now, but to put it on the best path I’d want these decisions from you:

What exam series are you targeting first: June 2026, November 2026, or later?

Are these students currently learning P3 for the first time, revising after instruction, or preparing close to the exam?

What is their current baseline: approximate P3 marks, predicted grades, or common weak topics?

Will students use this in class, for homework, or both?

Will they have laptops/tablets, or does this need to work well on phones?

Are you comfortable collecting anonymized pre/post assessment data so we can measure actual learning?

How much self-marking do you trust students to do, and how much teacher moderation do you want?

Which route are your students taking for A Level: P1/P3/P4/P5 or P1/P3/P5/P6?

Are you allowed to share the canonical paper/mark-scheme image assets with colleagues/students through this app, or does deployment need to stay local/private?

The project is absolutely worth continuing. The core idea is right. The next step is to make it less like a polished prototype with strong first regions, and more like a measured P3 learning system where every piece of content exists to produce assessment improvement.